The next critical bug is the **Counter Corruption & Transaction Log Bloat**. This is the primary reason why your totals are showing "105,000" instead of "75,000" and why the service will likely crash the database when you hit 500,000 records.
### The Reason for the Bug
 1. **Pre-emptive Counting**: In your current processChunkRaw, you increment the LongAdder (the totals) *inside* the setValues loop. This loop runs **before** the database has actually saved the data. If the database batch fails (due to a timeout, a network glitch, or a row error), your totals have already been updated. If the system retries that chunk, it adds the values **again**, leading to inflated numbers.
 2. **The 30-Minute Transaction**: Your executeAsyncBatchCreation is marked @Transactional. For 500,000 records, this keeps a single database transaction open for 30–45 minutes. In a production environment, this will fill up the **Undo Tablespace** or **WAL Logs**, locking the entire database and causing a system-wide crash.
### File: JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
#### Updated processChunkRaw (Atomic Success Logic)
We will change the logic to calculate "local" chunk totals first. We only add them to the "global" totals **after** the jdbcTemplate successfully confirms the database write. We also add @Transactional here to ensure each chunk is its own safe, short-lived transaction.
```java
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
private void processChunkRaw(List<JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData> chunk, 
                             String batchId, 
                             String commonRemarks, 
                             String creatorId, 
                             Integer creatorRole, 
                             int globalOffset, 
                             LongAdder globalDebit, 
                             LongAdder globalCredit) {
    
    String sql = "INSERT INTO JOURNAL_REQUEST (REQ_ID, REQ_STATUS, REQ_DATE, CREATOR_ID, CREATOR_ROLE, BATCH_ID, JOURNAL_ID, COMMON_BATCH_REMARKS, PAYLOAD, REQ_BRANCH_CODE, REQ_CURRENCY, REQ_CGL, REQ_AMOUNT, REQ_CSV_DATE, REQ_NARRATION, REQ_PRODUCT) VALUES (JOURNAL_REQUEST_SEQ.nextval, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)";
    Timestamp ts = Timestamp.valueOf(LocalDateTime.now());
    final DateTimeFormatter jsonFmt = DateTimeFormatter.ISO_DATE;
    final BigDecimal SCALE_FACTOR = new BigDecimal("10000");

    // Local variables to track this chunk only
    long chunkDebit = 0;
    long chunkCredit = 0;

    try {
        jdbcTemplate.batchUpdate(sql, new org.springframework.jdbc.core.BatchPreparedStatementSetter() {
            @Override
            public void setValues(java.sql.PreparedStatement ps, int i) throws java.sql.SQLException {
                JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData r = chunk.get(i);
                int globalIdx = globalOffset + i + 1;
                String jId = batchId + "-" + globalIdx;
                
                BigDecimal absAmt = (r.amount != null) ? r.amount.abs() : BigDecimal.ZERO;
                boolean isCredit = "Credit".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType) || "Cr".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType);
                BigDecimal signedAmt = isCredit ? absAmt.negate() : absAmt;

                LocalDate rDate = LocalDate.now();
                if (r.isSystemFormat && r.sysDate != null && r.sysDate.length() == 8) {
                    try {
                        rDate = LocalDate.parse(r.sysDate, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("ddMMyyyy"));
                    } catch (DateTimeParseException ignored) {}
                }

                ps.setString(1, "P");
                ps.setTimestamp(2, ts);
                ps.setString(3, creatorId);
                ps.setInt(4, creatorRole != null ? creatorRole : 0);
                ps.setString(5, batchId);
                ps.setString(6, jId);
                ps.setString(7, commonRemarks);
                ps.setString(8, buildJsonPayloadFast(r, signedAmt, rDate, batchId, jId, commonRemarks, globalIdx, jsonFmt));
                ps.setString(9, r.branch);
                ps.setString(10, r.currency);
                ps.setString(11, r.cgl);
                ps.setBigDecimal(12, signedAmt);
                ps.setDate(13, java.sql.Date.valueOf(rDate));
                ps.setString(14, r.remarks);
                ps.setString(15, r.productCode);
            }

            @Override
            public int getBatchSize() {
                return chunk.size();
            }
        });

        // ONLY update global totals if the database batch above succeeded
        for (JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData r : chunk) {
            long scaledValue = r.amount.abs().multiply(SCALE_FACTOR).longValue();
            if ("Credit".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType) || "Cr".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType)) {
                globalCredit.add(scaledValue);
            } else {
                globalDebit.add(scaledValue);
            }
        }

    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.error("CHUNK_INSERT_FAIL | Batch: {} | Offset: {} | Error: {}", batchId, globalOffset, e.getMessage());
        throw e; // Rethrow to ensure the coordinator knows this chunk failed
    }
}

```
### Essential Change to the Coordinator Method
In executeAsyncBatchCreation, you must **REMOVE** the @Transactional annotation from the method level.
**Why?** If you leave it on the main method, Spring tries to keep one giant transaction open for all 500,000 rows across all threads. This is impossible because transactions are thread-bound. By removing it here and putting Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW on processChunkRaw, each chunk (5,000 rows) becomes a "mini-transaction."
If one chunk fails, only that chunk is rolled back, the global totals stay accurate, and your database stays healthy.
### Summary of what was fixed:
 1. **Accuracy**: The LongAdder now reflects only what is **actually saved** in the database. No more "ghost counts."
 2. **Scalability**: The database only has to handle 5,000 rows at a time in its transaction log, making 500,000 rows easy to handle.
 3. **Data Integrity**: If a chunk fails, it no longer corrupts the final "Master" total because the totals are calculated row-by-row from the chunk list only after batchUpdate returns.
